          "title": "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "ac211cfe-4a70-5875-88f6-2b08996fd808",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the third row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are in row echelon form.",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
